  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small sample of 146 simulated flights within a single metropolitan area and assume a direct, one-to-one replacement of helicopter trips.",
      "what": "This study simulated the one-to-one replacement of 146 intra-metropolitan helicopter flights in the S\u0000ao Paulo Metropolitan Region with electric vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL) aircraft. Using flight trajectories constrained by local helicopter routes and simulated in the BlueSky air traffic simulator, the model calculated energy use and carbon dioxide equivalent emissions. The full substitution of these flights under a conflict-resolution scenario with a 60-second lookahead time avoided 10.27 tonnes of CO2e, representing a mean relative emission reduction of 92.11% in Monte Carlo sensitivity analyses.",
      "why": "It provides a transparent, simulation-based procedure to quantify the potential carbon credits and emission reductions of urban air mobility, demonstrating that the climate benefits of eVTOLs are highly sensitive to local electricity-grid carbon intensity and baseline helicopter fuel efficiency."
    }
  },
